// @check
// CONTRACT: the DISPLAY renderer GROUPS an array's element when the `[]` would otherwise
// re-bind. An array of a union is `(A | B)[]`; bare, `A | B[]` is `A | (B[])` — a union of
// `A` with a list of `B`, a DIFFERENT type that parses perfectly well.
//
// THIS FILE IS THE HEADLINE OF THAT DEFECT, AND IT IS NOT A WORDING COMPLAINT. Master
// prints, for the line below:
//
//   cannot assign {meow: i32} | {woof: i32}[] to 'bad' of type {meow: i32} | {woof: i32}[]
//
// — the two operands render CHARACTER-FOR-CHARACTER identically while being genuinely
// different types, so the compiler reports that a type cannot be assigned to itself. There
// is nothing a reader can do with that message. With the element grouped, the source side
// renders `({meow: i32} | {woof: i32})[]` and the two sides are told apart.
//
// THE ERROR IS THE PIN, AND IT IS A CONTROL THAT FAILS IN THE BROKEN CONFIGURATION: master
// cannot produce this string, because master has no spelling that distinguishes the two
// sides. It is a message pin rather than a `@log` one because this slice moves diagnostic
// TEXT and nothing else — emitted modules are byte-identical corpus-wide, so no program's
// output can witness the change.
//
// THE ANONYMOUS UNION IS LOAD-BEARING. `u` is un-annotated, so the array literal mints its
// own union of its own inline shapes — an arena index no declaration registered, which no
// nominal leg can name (`display-render-round-trips.vl`'s residue). A `type Animal = Cat |
// Dog` annotation would render `Animal[]` on both sides and hide the grouping question.
// @error cannot assign ({meow: i32} | {woof: i32})[] to 'bad' of type {meow: i32} | {woof: i32}[]

function main() {
  const u = [{ meow: 1 }, { woof: 2 }]
  const bad: {meow: i32} | {woof: i32}[] = u
  print(1)
}
main()
